WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.060
Fix a prime p and a root of unity
eta whose order is coprime to p.

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:10.680
Let A be the integer ring generated by
eta, localized at one prime above p:

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:13.300
denominators outside
that prime are allowed.

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:17.610
We use the cyclotomic facts
supplied by Lemma two point one.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:20.600
The coefficient ring is unramified at p,

00:00:20.840 --> 00:00:24.790
with integer valuation
normalized by v of p equals one.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:30.280
Suppose F times K equals p
to the e times b on a cycle

00:00:30.280 --> 00:00:35.020
of length p to the e, with
e at least one and b a unit.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:38.090
For a primitive p-th root rho,

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:42.160
our targets are the ratios
F of rho over F of one,

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:45.110
and K of rho over K of one.

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:49.380
Both denominators are nonzero,
but may have zero residue.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:51.850
Although rho reduces to one,

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:56.140
dividing two zero residues
would not justify either ratio.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:02.960
The local ring A with rho adjoined has
maximal ideal generated by rho minus one.

00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:08.160
A nonzero residue therefore means
a unit. First take e equal to one.

00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:13.730
The nonnegative integer valuations
of F of one and K of one add to one.

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:17.300
At least one is zero;
suppose F of one is a unit.

00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:22.250
Its ratio safely reduces to one.
The two ratios multiply to one,

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:27.873
since the evaluated products at
rho and at one both equal p b.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:32.750
The other ratio is the inverse,
also a unit of residue one.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:39.040
For e at least two, evaluate at a
primitive p to the e-th root xi.

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:45.600
The valuations of F of xi and K of
xi are nonnegative and add to e.

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:49.180
They may be fractional, but
one must be at least one.

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:52.680
Relabel the factors so that it is F of xi.

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:59.160
Divide F by the monic cyclotomic
polynomial: F equals Q times Phi,

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:02.720
plus R, with degree of R less than D.

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:05.960
Here D is the product of p minus one

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:09.700
and p raised to the exponent e minus one.

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:15.270
At xi, Phi vanishes, so R of
xi has valuation at least one.

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:18.960
The first D powers of xi form an A basis.

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:23.570
The local maximal ideal is
generated by xi minus one.

00:02:24.060 --> 00:02:27.920
The supplied input makes every
remainder coefficient divisible by p.

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:32.360
This is an arithmetic basis
fact, not complex magnitude.

00:02:33.320 --> 00:02:38.260
Now project to the cycle of length p to
the e minus one; call this projection q.

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:43.140
Each of the p terms of Phi
becomes one, so q of Phi equals p.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:46.740
Both parts of q of F are divisible by p.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:52.960
Define F prime as q of F divided
by p, and K prime as q of K.

00:02:53.620 --> 00:02:56.920
Their product is p to
the e minus one times b.

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:00.060
Cancelling p is valid
in the free A module.

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:03.460
This is exact division
before reducing residues.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:06.740
Both inputs, one and rho,

00:03:06.740 --> 00:03:10.460
satisfy the shorter cycle relation.
Projection changes neither evaluation.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:14.600
Dividing F by p cancels from
numerator and denominator,

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:19.590
so F prime of rho over F prime of
one equals F of rho over F of one.

00:03:19.820 --> 00:03:21.720
The ratio for K is unchanged too.

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:27.260
Repeat the shortening. The cycle exponent
and scalar exponent decrease together,

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:31.860
while both ratios remain unchanged.
We may divide different factors at

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:35.020
different steps. Stop when
the exponent reaches one.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:40.100
The base case then proves that both
original ratios are units of residue one.

00:03:40.780 --> 00:03:46.650
Apply this to f times f star equals u
squared, with u odd and greater than one.

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:51.740
Fix p dividing u. Its cycle
component has order p to the e,

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:55.320
where e is exactly the
exponent of p in u squared.

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:59.860
Evaluate the other prime components first
and localize their coefficient ring.

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:02.400
Their root orders are coprime to p.

00:04:02.800 --> 00:04:07.240
Write b as u squared divided by
p to the e; it is a unit here.

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:10.100
The remaining product
is p to the e times b.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:13.440
The exponents match, so
our comparison applies.

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:20.260
For a subset S not containing p,
the ratio f at zeta sub S union p,

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:26.260
divided by f at zeta sub S, is a unit
of residue one at every prime above p.

00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:28.720
Its inverse has the same property.

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:33.120
This conclusion belongs to the
ratios, not to the individual values.

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:37.740
These comparisons will control the
denominators in the alternating product.
